CloudBurrow patch 54 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1459).

The bug: an UPDATE or DELETE whose WHERE (or SET) has a subquery of
several FROM items, such as
  DELETE ds.t WHERE id IN (SELECT u.id FROM ds.u u JOIN ds.w w USING (k))
failed 400 "sqlite3: SQL logic error: ambiguous column name: k". The
formatter writes an UPDATE's or DELETE's column references by their bare
names, which SQLite's UPDATE and DELETE need for the changed table, and so
it wrote the subquery's too: two scans with a column of one name could no
longer be told apart. A query (and a MERGE) names its scans' columns by
column ID (k#3, k#5).

The fix: an UPDATE or DELETE records its table scan's column IDs; a
subquery inside it is written with column IDs, as a query is, except for
the changed table's columns (a correlated reference, t.id), which keep
their names, and SQLite resolves them to the outer table.

